p.src:
A hadron is an atom-like bound system made of quarks and gluons, but its scale is much smaller: $R_{\rm hadron}\sim10^{-15}\ {\rm m},
\qquad
t_{\rm transit}^{\rm hadron}\sim10^{-23}\ {\rm s}.$ Quarks cross the hadron on essentially the same time scale, and strongly decaying excited hadrons also live for roughly $t_{\rm orbit}^{\rm hadron}
\sim t_{\rm decay}^{\rm hadron}
\sim10^{-23}\ {\rm s}.$ A struck hadron may vibrate and emit a pion almost as quickly as causality allows. There is no seven- or nine-order-of-magnitude separation like the one inside an atom.

